# core.autocrlf=true is set on the Windows machines this is developed on, which means a fresh
# checkout gets CRLF working-tree copies of everything. That is harmless for source — and not
# harmless at all for these, because `release.sh deploy` scp's the WORKING TREE copy of a
# script to a Linux server and runs it there. A CRLF shell script fails on its first line with
#
#     $'\r': command not found
#
# which reads as a broken script rather than a line-ending problem. Pin them.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.service   text eol=lf
*.env       text eol=lf
*.env.example text eol=lf
*.sql       text eol=lf
*.conf      text eol=lf
